    "sources": [
        {
            "subject": "attitude",
            "status": "estimate",
            "value": "flown like the ISS: +X (Tianhe's axis, node forward) along velocity, +Z nadir, labs along the orbit normal",
            "source": "No published flight attitude found. With the labs' arrays turning about the labs' common axis (below), that axis must lie across the orbit plane to track the Sun once per orbit; Tianhe's forward/nadir/aft ports serve visiting vehicles (Wikipedia, Tianhe). Estimate."
        },
        {
            "subject": "station",
            "status": "sourced",
            "value": "root group: Tianhe + the two laboratory modules in a T",
            "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tiangong space station' / 'Tianhe core module' / 'Wentian module' (accessed 2026-09-24): 'T-shaped', Wentian starboard and Mengtian port of Tianhe's forward docking hub."
        },
        {
            "subject": "alpha",
            "status": "derived",
            "value": "each lab's rear boom turns about the labs' common axis, tracking the Sun (first of the arrays' two axes)",
            "source": "China Space Report, 'Tiangong' (chinaspacereport.wordpress.com): the lab modules' wings are 'two-axis steerable to track the Sun', 'mounted on a large boom with its centre attached to the rear end of the module'. Assigning the first axis to the module axis (as the ISS SARJ) is DERIVED from that layout."
        },
        {
            "subject": "beta",
            "status": "derived",
            "value": "each wing turns about its own long axis (second axis); the two wings of a lab share one line (2 pivots)",
            "source": "China Space Report, 'Tiangong' (chinaspacereport.wordpress.com) (two-axis steerable). Axis assignment derived, as for alpha."
        },
        {
            "subject": "tianhe_node",
            "status": "estimate",
            "value": "2.8 m sphere at Tianhe's forward end; range 2.5-3.0 m",
            "source": "eoPortal, 'Tiangong Space Station': Tianhe = 'the node cabin, the life control cabin, which is divided into the large and small column segments, and the resource cabin'. Section sizes are not published; node and small column taken at ~2.8 m."
        },
        {
            "subjects": [
                "tianhe_small_section",
                "tianhe_large_section"
            ],
            "status": "derived",
            "value": "4.0 m x 2.8 m small column + 9.8 m x 4.2 m large column/resource cabin: 16.6 m overall with the node",
            "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tiangong space station' / 'Tianhe core module' / 'Wentian module' (accessed 2026-09-24) and eoPortal, 'Tiangong Space Station': total length 16.6 m, maximum diameter 4.2 m. The split between sections is an estimate (range +-1.5 m)."
        },
        {
            "subjects": [
                "wentian",
                "mengtian"
            ],
            "status": "sourced",
            "value": "17.9 m x 4.2 m each, docked 0.4 m off the node",
            "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tiangong space station' / 'Tianhe core module' / 'Wentian module' (accessed 2026-09-24): both 17.9 m long, 4.2 m diameter. Station 'diameter ~39 m' = the two labs end to end (39.4 m here)."
        },
        {
            "subjects": [
                "wentian_array_boom",
                "mengtian_array_boom"
            ],
            "status": "estimate",
            "value": "1 x 1 x 3 m box at each lab's rear end",
            "source": "China Space Report, 'Tiangong' (chinaspacereport.wordpress.com): 'a large boom with its centre attached to the rear end of the module'. Size not published."
        },
        {
            "subjects": [
                "wentian_wing_nadir",
                "wentian_wing_zenith",
                "mengtian_wing_nadir",
                "mengtian_wing_zenith"
            ],
            "status": "derived",
            "value": "four 27 x 4.1 m flexible wings (110 m2 each), two per lab, 57.6 m tip to tip",
            "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tiangong space station' / 'Tianhe core module' / 'Wentian module' (accessed 2026-09-24) (Wentian) and eoPortal, 'Tiangong Space Station': each lab's pair has a 'wingspan of over 55 m and an energy collection area of 110 m2' per array; Space.com: each array 'nearly 30 m long'. Width = area / length (derived). Flexible GaAs arrays: solar_cell_flex / solar_array_flex_back (backlit transmission) - estimate for the substrate. Tianhe's own two small one-axis wings are OMITTED: CMSE's design moves them to the labs' short trusses once the labs arrive; where they are now is unverified."
        },
        {
            "subject": "cn_module_blanket",
            "status": "estimate",
            "value": "diffuse albedo 0.6, white thermal blankets; range 0.45-0.8",
            "source": "Tiangong's modules appear white in CMSE imagery; no published reflectance."
        },
        {
            "subject": "boom_metal",
            "status": "estimate",
            "value": "albedo 0.3, F0 0.3, roughness 0.45 (the ISS truss material)",
            "source": "No published reflectance."
        }
    ],
